00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.570 align:start position:0%
 
a touching tribute to the late Chadwick  Boseman that's also a thrilling new  installment in the MCU Saga black  panther wakanda forever was a tall order  but Marvel Studios pulled it off here's  how  wakanda forever is the 30th feature film  in the Marvel Cinematic Universe by now  audiences are so familiar with the mcu's  opening credits format we could all  probably reproduce the moving letters  and rousing music from memory but Marvel  has been experimenting with its  recognizable logo as of late the titles  for Thor love and thunder swapped the  traditional MCU soundtrack for a guns  and roses-esque power ballad version and  werewolf By Night bypassed them all  together for its own short Snappy and  brightly colored introduction but Marvel  Studios opening credits have never  looked or sounded like they do in  wakanda forever and they've never been  used for such a purpose before the  standard sequencies hand-drawn Marvel  Comics illustrations morph into live  action Marvel Studios film clips all on  the surface of 3D block letters as each  new superhero is introduced they get  added to the next entries Graphics but  this time rather than the entire stable  of Marvel superheroes being represented  every still and clip is of Chadwick  Boseman as t'challa for the first time  there's no music underscoring the  visuals it's 30 seconds of respectful  silence as the words Marvel Studios fix  themselves in the center of the screen  they briefly glow purple the shade of  the heart-shaped herb before Fading Into  the silver and black of t'challa's black  panther suit there's nothing especially  deep about superficial changes to a  corporate logo but they help to set the  tone for the film that's about to unfold  Bozeman's tragic death in August of 2020  meant that black panther wakanda forever  had to be Rewritten from a script that  was already in the works fans and  filmmakers alike debated about the best  way forward for the franchise given the  choice between recasting the main  character or elevating the status of one  of the supporting players director and  co-writer Ryan coogler opted for the  latter and got to work retooling the  script the movie opens with a  heart-wrenching scene in which shuri is  racing against the clock to improve the  formula for her synthetic heart-shaped  her but before she can test out the  unlikely cure her mother Queen raimonda  announces that t'challa has gone to his  ancestors The Black Panther's stats from  an illness that modern medicine even  wakandas can't treat is an obvious  allusion to Bozeman's own death from  colon cancer at the age of 43. it was  both smart screenwriting and  compassionate collaboration that led to  this decision as Kugler explained to The  Hollywood Reporter Bozeman's family  consulted on the film which ensured that  the fun metal product would show the  late actor proper deference other  franchises have had to deal with similar  issues though not to this extent for  example Carrie Fisher's death between  production on The Last Jedi and the rise  of Skywalker deeply affected the final  chapter of the Star Wars saga she was  expected to play a larger role in the  third movie in the sequel trilogy but  her death changed those plans by not  straying too far from the truth wakanda  forever allows us to mourn Bozeman and  t'challa at the same time without  trivializing the tragedy  the primary way we and the characters in  wakanda Forever mourn the loss of the  real actor and the fictional King is  through t'challa's emotional and  elaborate funeral festivities in Captain  America Civil War t'challa talks to  Natasha about how he's processing his  own father's death after the bombing in  Vienna in my culture death  it's not the end  since wakandans believe in an afterlife  and a spiritual realm the sad occasion  of the King's funeral is both an  opportunity to grieve and a celebration  of his life pulling from African  cultural and religious Heritage will  cause his people Gathering Gauzy white  ceremonial clothing there's dancing  singing and mourning there are plenty of  nods to Chadwick boseman's vaunted place  in the MCU as well  during the funeral we see a shot of two  drummers playing in front of a mural of  King t'challa wakandan letters surround  a black and white painting of the Fallen  Black Panther's face production designer  Hannah beachler actually developed a  written language for wakanda in the  first film according to Indie wire from  what we can see some of those characters  are used here to spell out the king  lives and the panther forever with us  the mural gives filmmakers a way to show  t'challa's face without Bozeman's  physical presence and without resorting  to flashbacks too often but it's also a  reference to something that really  happened in the wake of the Beloved  actor's death Disneyland commissioned an  official mural of Chadwick Boseman that  was displayed at Downtown Disney and it  eventually moved to a children's  hospital where it will remain as a  permanent installation other murals  began to pop up around the country to  honor the man who had been a hero and an  inspiration to so many the mural in  wakanda Forever is an acknowledgment of  t'challa's importance to the wakandans  and of Bozeman's importance to multiple  communities  the wakandan alphabet can be spotted  throughout both black panther films in  wakanda Forever Riri Williams is taken  back to the secretive African nation and  sees their Rich culture and advanced  technology for the first time as she's  given a sort of VIP tour the Durham  milage stand guard in front of  rectangular columns which are lit from  within and decorated with wakandan  characters each one contains a word or  phrase and the five that are most  visible during riri's visit spell in  power t'challa our hero and honor  forever it appears the designers added  this detail as an Easter egg and an  extra way to stamp Bozeman's memory onto  the set  that nobody can replace t'challa's king  or his Black Panther in the movie  becomes a meta commentary on the idea  that nobody could really replace  Chadwick Boseman in the role wakanda is  without its protector because Eric  killmonger AKA and jadaka burned the  reserves of the heart-shaped herbs so  that no one could challenge him during  the events of the original film burn it  all  as wakanda forever begins literally no  one can replace t'challa since the  enhanced powers of the Black Panther  aren't available to them without their  vibranium-laced plants Queen ramonda is  acting as Regent sure he seems more  interested in science than politics and  mbaku is content to lead the Jabari and  be an ally of the wakandan crown and  since shuri has given up on the idea of  creating a synthetic heart-shaped herb  everybody else has given up on the idea  of there ever being a black panther  again indeed there isn't one until the  climax of wakanda forever narratively  this works well to give Bozeman his due  and to build anticipation for the moment  when shuri finally takes the mantle  one of the reason Chadwick boseman's  loss hit so hard among moviegoers who  didn't know him personally was because  the actor gave off such a warm authentic  and eminently likable energy he brought  that energy to t'challa who was among  the more stoic characters in the MCU  black panther didn't get quips and gags  like Iron Man Ant-Man Star-Lord or  Doctor Strange he was the mcu's straight  man even more so than Captain America in  stark contrast killmonger his cousin and  antagonist in Black Panther got to chew  the scenery while t'challa remained  Regal and restrained Michael B Jordan  gets to come back in one of the mcu's  most purposeful cameos as killmacher who  didn't make it out of the last movie  alive when shuri enters the ancestral  plane she wants to see her brother or  her mother instead she sees killmacher  who shares the rage she has at having  lost her family it would have been  unrealistic for killmonger a notoriously  confrontational and viciously honest  character to have softened too much  toward his former rival what njidaka has  to say to shuri about her brother is  completely in keeping with his character  he admits that t'challa was a good man  but warned shuri that he was too Noble  for his own good shuri takes this advice  to Heart Waging War against the talocon  until she realizes her name was thirst  for vengeance can only end in Calamity  for both of their Nations  [Music]  after the climactic battle in wakanda  Forever shuri heads to Haiti to burn her  funeral clothes and finally grieve her  brother's passing in peace shots of the  fibers burning are intercut with  breathtaking shots of shuri and profile  beginning to cry when she closes her  eyes we see a montage of her memories of  t'challa made up of Bozeman's highlights  from his four film tenure in the MCU  these clips Crossfade into the end  credits accompanied by Rihanna's new  original song Lift Me Up the lyrics of  which include the repeated phrase keep  me in the warmth of your love when you  depart it's undoubtedly the quietest  calmest and most Earnest ending in MCU  history this symbolic end to shuri's  morning period is also incredibly  cathartic for the audience who's  probably dabbing the tears from their  eyes when the final title card appears  reading dedicated to our friend Chadwick  Boseman it's a beautiful  contribution to Chadwick's Legacy  usually Marvel's mid and end credit  Stingers are reserved for teasing new  projects and making tongue-in-cheek  jokes wakanda forever uses its lone and  longer than usual mid-credits scene to  touchingly and gracefully transition the  Black Panther franchise within a  franchise onto the next leg of its  Journey while shuri's still having a  moment on the beach Nakia walks down to  where she's sitting with a young boy she  introduces him as her son and shuri's  nephew Toussaint as it happens t'challa  prepared Nakia and their secret son for  his death and the family of two has been  staying away from whatever drama will  await the Young Prince when he comes of  age shuri is heartened to find out that  Rwanda got to meet her grandson before  she died then the adorable kid tells his  entry that Toussaint is his Haitian name  but his real name is t'challa this new  and unexpected mid-credits information  is an elegant solution to the problem of  whether or not to cast a new king  t'challa introducing the character's son  allows Bozeman to Remain the sole actor  to portray King t'challa son of t'chaka  while the next Generations t'challa can  eventually become a part of the MCU in  his own right  [Music]

